__pycache__/
*.pyc

# --- data artifacts: never versioned ----------------------------------------
# The repo holds the PIPELINE, not its output. Derived datasets are large and
# reproducible by running data-pipeline/ (see its README), so they stay local.
#
# This is not only a tidiness rule. GitHub enforces a hard 100 MB per-file limit
# and rejects the whole push if any object exceeds it -- data/dyad_dataset*.csv
# run to 506-784 MB each and made the push unrejectable-but-unsendable until
# they were untracked.
*.csv
*.parquet
*.pq
*.feather
*.npy
*.npz
*.pkl
*.h5
*.nc

# EXCEPTION: the 2015->2020 municipality crosswalk is a pipeline INPUT, not an
# output -- 5 KB, hand-verified against the INEGI catalogo, and that verification
# work must not be lost. data-pipeline/.gitignore carries the same exception.
!data-pipeline/data/raw/crosswalk_municipios_2015_2020.csv

# --- model artifacts --------------------------------------------------------
*.pt
*.pth
*.ckpt
*.safetensors

# --- run outputs ------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything under models/runs/ is regenerated by re-running the models: the
# training logs, the per-run figure directories, the checkpoints and the gate
# embeddings. Committing them means the repository accumulates the output of
# every exploratory run, and a reader cannot tell which one produced the paper.
#
# No model figures are versioned anywhere. There used to be an exception here for
# models/gnn_figures/, which held eight figures from the model's earlier BRAZILIAN
# application (the maps drew Brazil, the gate plots were labelled in BRL). They
# were deleted rather than left to be mistaken for Mexican results, and the
# exception went with them. Regenerate figures with analysis/maps.py.
models/runs/

# The modelling splits are generated by models/make_splits.py from the pipeline
# output. The CSVs are already covered by *.csv above; this catches the manifest
# that records how they were built.
/data/splits_manifest.md
